fault_phenomenon.description:
## Summary

`ThetaStar::generatePath` expands `curr_data` **before** popping the next node from the open set. The loop condition is `!queue_.empty()`.

After the last remaining node is popped, the queue is empty, so the loop exits **without**:

1. calling `setNeighbors` on that node
2. checking `isGoal` on that node

If that node is the only 8-connected gateway out of a pocket, or is the goal itself, planning returns failure even though a collision-free grid path exists.

This is independent of `how_many_corners` (8-connected expansion is already used).

Relevant code: https://github.com/ros-navigation/navigation2/blob/main/nav2_theta_star_planner/src/theta_star.cpp

```cpp
while (!queue_.empty()) {
  // ...
  if (isGoal(*curr_data)) {
    break;
  }

  resetParent(curr_data);
  setNeighbors(curr_data);

  curr_data = queue_.top();
  queue_.pop();
}

if (queue_.empty()) {
  raw_path.clear();
  return false;
}
```

## Minimal repro

Map with two adjacent free cells. Start `(0,0)`, goal `(1,0)`. A 4-connected path exists.

With default weights (`w_euc_cost=1`, `w_traversal_cost=2`):

1. `curr = start` (start is still in the queue)
2. Expand start → goal is pushed
3. Pop start again (`f(start) < f(goal)`)
4. Expand start again, pop goal → queue is empty, `curr = goal`
5. Loop exits because `queue_.empty()`
6. `isGoal(goal)` is never checked → `return false`

The same false failure happens on a 2x2 map whose only connection is the diagonal (one 8-connected step).

An open 3x3 map often succeeds, because other open-set nodes remain when the goal is popped. That is why this is easy to miss in typical demos.

## Real-world case

On a recorded global costmap, the robot was in a small 4-connected pocket (~147 cells). The only exit was one diagonal cell with cost 221 (both orthogonal neighbors were 253).

That gateway cell had a high traversal cost, so it was the last node popped from the open set and was never expanded. Theta* reported no path, even though an 8-connected path with cost `< 253` existed.

## Expected

Pop a node, then test `isGoal` / run `setNeighbors`. An empty queue should mean “no node left to expand”, not “skip the node we just popped”.

## Suggested fix

Use standard pop-then-expand:

```cpp
while (!queue_.empty()) {
  tree_node * curr_data = queue_.top();
  queue_.pop();

  if (isGoal(*curr_data)) {
    backtrace(raw_path, curr_data);
    clearQueue();
    return true;
  }

  resetParent(curr_data);
  setNeighbors(curr_data);
}

raw_path.clear();
return false;
```

With this change, the two-cell map, the diagonal 2x2 map, a 3-cell corridor, and the recorded pocket costmap all find a path.

